  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 324 AM MDT Fri Sep 18 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nA somewhat elongated upper level low across the Pacific NW and \nvicinity will slowly shift eastward through the day today. Although \nforcing from this approaching upper system will result in showers \nand thunderstorms developing rather early in the convective cycle, \ncool temperatures aloft will still yield sufficiently steep lapse \nrates for a few stronger thunderstorms. Areas where shear ends up \nbeing maximized will have a threat for marginally large hail. \nOtherwise the main threat will be for slow moving, heavy rain \nproducing thunderstorms through the day today. \n\nDifluent upper level flow on the northeast side of the upper level \nsystem look to combine with lingering instability and forcing from \nthe approaching trough to result in an extended stretch of rain, \nmostly near and south of a line from Helena to Lewistown southward \nto a line from Dillon to Big Sky tonight through the day Saturday. \nThis prolonged stretch of rain will result in concern for excessive \nrainfall, particularly over burn scars, but for urban areas as well. \n\nPrecipitation slowly exits eastward late Saturday night and Sunday \nmorning as a messy/weak upper level ridge builds in Sunday into \nMonday. Much of next week looks dry, though weak disturbances cannot \nbe ruled out, which would be capable of producing spotty light \nprecipitation at times. -AM\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nRain through Saturday: \n\nConfidence is increasing in a concentrated area of moderate \nrainfall, mainly along and south of a Helena to Lewistown line \nsouthward through a Dillon to Big Sky line. Over 48 hours ending \nSunday morning the corridor along I-90 east of Butte through Bozeman \nhas around a 90% probability to receive 1 inch of rain. These \nprobabilities slowly decrease as you go further north and south of \nthis corridor.\n\nThis rainfall looks to be partially convective driven and partially \nstratiform in nature. The risk for excessive rainfall leading to \nflash flooding will be highest while convective processes are \ndominant, which will be primarily in the afternoon and evening. That \nsaid, burn scars will be susceptible to flash flooding at any time \ngiven the significantly lower precipitation amount thresholds. -AM",
